{
  "assessment": "The source provides a clear quantitative cluster: trapped-atom exponential decay versus lattice hold time for the 3D and 1D lattices, with fitted lifetimes of 54 ms and 220 ms respectively. The reviewer figure evidence adds three labeled hold-time positions (100, 200, 300 ms) that lie within the displayed fit segments, but these are not asserted to be the paper's exact sampling grid. The source does not provide the raw atom-number data points or the fitted amplitudes, so the quantitative form can only be expressed as an exponential decay with the given lifetimes and an unspecified initial amplitude. The control is hold time, with a source-supported domain of t_hold >= 75 ms (continuous interval from 75 ms upward, though the upper bound is not specified). The readout is the number of trapped atoms, defined via fluorescence imaging after 1 ms time of flight. The statistical basis is exponential fitting excluding t_hold < 75 ms. Uncertainties on the lifetimes are not reported. The case is authoring-ready because there is at least one numeric control (hold time), one numeric readout (atom number), a quantitative mechanism (exponential decay), and an objective source-grounded response within an explicit domain.",
